            <div class="post">
               <h5 class="posthead"><a href="/blogs/ryanc/archive/2007/10/25/wpf-controls-tabindex-moving-to-the-first-input-control.aspx">WPF Controls TabIndex: Moving to the First Input Control</a></h5>

               <p>Here&#39;s one of those real-world situations that no one bothers to demo.&nbsp; If you are building a form or have some sort of input your gathering from the user, you&#39;ll likely want the first input control Focused initially.&nbsp; You have a few ways to do this (in ascending order of recommendation):</p> <ol> <li>Call Focus() on the first control explicitly (meh).  <li>Iterate the controls collection and find the&nbsp;lowest&nbsp;TabIndex&nbsp;(terrible <a href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Code_smell">code smell</a>).  <li>Do the following in the Load event:</li></ol> <blockquote><pre class="code"><span>this</span>.MoveFocus(<span>new</span> <span>TraversalRequest</span>(<span>FocusNavigationDirection</span>.Next));</pre></blockquote>

